window.docContents[140].resolve({"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity":{"contents":"A traveling wave is the simplest change of independent variables: the single\ncoordinate  follows a profile moving at constant speed , so any\nequation the profile satisfies is an ordinary differential equation in that\ncoordinate. The construction imposes no equation by itself; it is a total\nfunction of the profile and the speed, with time in coordinate  and\nspace in coordinate  of .\n\nThe traveling-wave construction sends a profile  and speed  to\n\nDifferentiating  in  and in  produces the same\n with factors  and , so the traveling ansatz is exactly\nadapted to the transport operator . The\nconclusion is a pointwise identity between totalized partial derivatives on\n.\n\nEvery differentiable traveling-wave profile satisfies the one-dimensional\ntransport equation .\n\nApply the chain rule to . It gives\n and . Therefore\n.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","header":"9.2.1.4. Traveling Waves and Similarity","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Reading-the-InfoView":{"contents":"Every #cas mode uses the same compact layout. The heading repeats the\ncommand as typed; Domain states the real coordinate space or closed interval;\nInput and Output state both shape and scalar type; further results read off\nthe output, such as the bounds of an enclosure, follow it; and Conditions,\nwhen present, records branch or denominator assumptions.\n\nThe proof-evidence section lists the propositions and theorem names\nthat Lean typechecked. Route metadata is separate and depends on the mode.\nFor integration, the displayed normalization passes explain the accepted\ncandidate's residual check; they do not record rejected primitive candidates.\nThe strategy list in a planner refusal names available methods; it does not\nrecord which methods ran.\n\nFor theorem goals, cas? and cega? display the suggested proof and a short\nfooter naming the selected route. Set cas.verbose to true locally\nto inspect the full evidence tree, with its checked propositions and separate\nplanner metadata; the same option adds the derivation trees to #cas reports.\n\nThe integration route returns that explanation with its completed proof.\nA primitive report identifies explicit verification, verification of a\ngoal-derived primitive, or bounded search. When the ordinary HasDerivAt\nfallback certifies the function already in the statement, its label is\nchecked primitive differentiation, since the function came from the\nstatement and was not found by search. A direct Mathlib calculus step names the theorem used and\nretains its analytic premises. The reporting layer checks those premise\nproofs separately from the proof of the original goal.\n\nThe suggested replay retains an explicit or goal-derived primitive when the\nroute has recorded the ordinary function. Replaying it checks the residual\nand domain requirements again. Direct Mathlib steps can replay the completed\nproof with exact. A search result without a recorded ordinary function\nreplays the integration tactic; its suggestion does not freeze an unrecorded\nsearch decision.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.8. Reading the InfoView","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Reading-the-InfoView"}});